{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/799626-divided-we-fall","id":799626,"title":"Divided we Fall","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-01-03T08:20:30-08:00","id":799626,"title":"Divided we Fall","content":"<span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\"><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">.. currently working on a Werewolf: the Apocalypse game. This is supposed to be a game focused on tragedy; in which the player characters are members of a sept destined to fall due to internal conflicts. <br /> <br /> The point is to explore these internal conflicts. Premier among them is the divide between breeds. There's a sizey population of Lupus who find it difficult to play nice with homids, let alone humans - a race that does a great job defiling the earth. This is where the whole role reversal aspect of the game comes to the fore, with the Lupus considering humanity as a whole as the enemy, wanting to treat people the way people treat animals. <br /> <br /> And, of course, noone really likes and respects the Metis. <br /> <br /> Tribal conflicts should be a pretty important thing as well - territorial struggles (Pure Ones vs. Wyrmcomers), tribes focusing on different priorities (Glasswalkers vs. Red Talons), conflicts over power and control (Silver Fangs vs. Shadow Lords), etc. <br /> <br /> The Wyrm's role in this setting is not to provide external conflict, but to embody the corruption inherent in both the werewolf race and humanity. Banes possess people, offer promises of power to werewolves, etc. <br /> <br /> Thoughts on how to make this cool?</div> </span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-03T15:18:37-08:00","id":799829,"title":"","content":"I like giving players contradictory missions, which would work well for you concept. Have them receive requests from both factions, but taking one alienates them from the other.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1619-derzhuzad","name":"Derzhuzad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1619_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-03T19:45:47-08:00","id":800051,"title":"","content":"I think you need some great NPCs to drive these conflicts and turn it into Shakespearean tragedy. You really need a Hamlet or Macbeth experience that at the end of the chronicle leaves the sept in tatters, most of the NPCs dead, and the PCs among the few survivors.<br /> <br /> Here are some ideas.<br /> <br /> 1) The main lupus antagonist/leader should not be a Red Talon. Make it some other tribe. That will complicate politics and make it harder for people to sideline the leader. It's easy to gain up on the Red Talons. It is much harder to do it to a Get of Fenris or Silver Fang lupus whose homid tribemates will still rally to his defense.<br /> <br /> 2) Don't be blatant about the real motivations of the NPCs who driving each of these conflicts. There should usually be a pretext that allows the NPC to get more allies or present his actions in a move favorable light. You want Tragic Heroes with tragic flaws, not outright villains. The individuals who will destroy the sept should originally be seen like heroes.<br /> <br /> 3) Give the NPCs a personal reason for the stances they take. That makes it much harder for PCs to resolve these plots. The Glass Walker is opposing the lupus not just because of &quot;philosophical differences&quot; but because the leader of the lupus belongs to a Pure Breed lineage that murdered one of his ancestors, and he absolutely knows that his ancestor (even though he cannot have the Ancestors background) wants revenge. Have the lupus or other NPC lead antagonist be told by a spirit that it is his destiny to become Sept Leader,and therefore he believes all his actions to achieve this is blessed by the gods. Have one of the other NPCs be dominated by his kinfolk parent who resents not having Changed and knowing that if he had changed, he would have lead the sept to glory so he is pushing his daughter to be the leader he would have been. Have long standing rivalries between different lineages of Garou who must keep up the rivalry/feud because to do otherwise would be dishonorable.<br /> <br /> 4) The PCs role in this will be like Fortinbras arriving in Denmark after everyone dies, or like Malcolm and Fleance in Macbeth - their main role is to come in after the sept tears itself apart. So they need to keep busy with other things than internal sept politics. They should be observing all this happening, but they their story should almost be separate from the intrigue. They should be involved tangentially in these plots.<br /> <br /> 5) It's very easy to make this railroady and that the PCs can't do anything. Don't make your intrigue dependent on any specific people. Always have a replacement who can resume the intrigue of their faction, but perhaps differently than before so it seems to the PCs that things have changed when they really haven't.<br /> <br /> 6) From my experience, PCs hate anyone who does not give them rewards or insults them, and likes any character who gives them rewards or praises them. It's a good idea early in the campaign that each of your lead antagonists do something beneficial for the PCs. This might be giving them a fetish as a reward, choosing to recount one of their deeds in person at a moot, or something else. They should obviously like the PCs, and this will probably be reciprocated. This will make it harder for the PCs to act against these NPCs early on. They will likely dither or act hesitantly rather than outright oppose them.<br /> <br /> 7) Have red herring villains in the sept which will divert the PCs attention to these people rather than the ones who will actually tear apart the sept. Don't go overboard, but have about 2-3 who can hold the attention of the PCs. When the sept begins to fall apart, they will go after these individuals instead. While one such NPC might be an actual traitor, it is also good to have an NPC who is actually heroic and is doing the right thing, but who the PCs will oppose because he is the biggest jerk in the entire sept who was offended by the PCs early on. Have the PCs hate this guy so they work to eliminate to him or thwart his plans, even though he is the one who is (ineffectually) trying to keep the sept together (perhaps by becoming the Sept Leader, and he is trying to undermine the NPC lead antagonists, but the PCs think he is being self-serving instead).<br /> <br /> This screams Shakespeare. So you need great characters to drive this. Don't hesitate to raid Shakespeare's plays for character ideas and factions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}